alth conditions by outlining safe food handling practices.DIETA ESPECIAL/NO CODIFICADA/PERSONALIZADA:A personalized diet plan for patients who cannot follow a standard diet due to various reasons, requiring assessment by nutrition professionals.DIETA OVOLACTOVEGETARIANA:A vegetarian diet that includes eggs and dairy products, designed to provide balanced nutrition while avoiding meat.RECOMENDACIONES GENERALES:General dietary recommendations for patients with esophagogastric diets, focusing on meal timing, portion sizes, and food choices.PROTECCI√ìN G√ÅSTRICA:A dietary approach aimed at minimizing mechanical and chemical stimulation of the stomach, often involving low-fiber and low-acid foods.PROTECCI√ìN BILIAR:A dietary strategy that reduces fat intake to support gallbladder health, often involving low-fat dairy and simple cooking methods.NUTRITIONAL CONTROL:Nutritional control for patients undergoing bariatric surgery aims to ensure adequate intake, limit complications, and address nutritional deficits.LIQUID DIET:The "LIQUID DIET" refers to a specific dietary phase that is commonly implemented following bariatric surgery. This diet involves the consumption of only liquids, which is crucial for aiding recovery and adaptation after the surgical procedure. The primary purpose of the liquid diet is to protect the surgical sites and facilitate healing. It is characterized by small volumes of liquid intake, ensuring that patients can gradually reintroduce food while minimizing the risk of complications. Additionally, a liquid diet can also be prescribed for patients with certain medical conditions, emphasizing its role in various therapeutic contexts. Overall, the liquid diet serves as an essential step in the post-operative care of bariatric surgery patients, focusing on liquid or liquid-form foods to support their recovery process.SOLID DIET:A solid diet is the final phase of dietary reintroduction after bariatric surgery, transitioning from liquids to solid foods.SUPPLEMENTAL NUTRITION:Supplemental nutrition includes additional nutritional products provided to patients to meet dietary needs post-surgery.YOGURT:Yogurt is a dairy product that serves as a nutritious option for patients, particularly those who are lactose intolerant. It is a fermented dairy product that not only provides essential calcium but also allows individuals to enjoy a source of nutrition while minimizing lactose intake.LEGUMES:Legumes are a category of plant foods recognized for their nutritional benefits, particularly their high protein and fiber content, which contribute positively to cardiovascular health. They are frequently highlighted in dietary guidelines, emphasizing their favorable phosphorus-to-protein ratio, making them suitable for renal patients. However, it is important to note that legumes are restricted in the diets of individuals who need to limit their potassium intake, as they contain high levels of potassium. Overall, legumes play a significant role in nutrition, but their consumption should be tailored to individual dietary needs, especially for those with specific health conditions.√ÅNGEL MANUEL CARACUEL GARC√çA:√Ångel Manuel Caracuel Garc√≠a is an author known for his work on the normalization of hospital food and nutrition.CAMPFR√çO FOOD GROUP:Campofr√≠o Food Group is a food company that specializes in the production and distribution of processed meat products. In addition to its core business, the company has also published works on hospital nutrition and food standards, indicating its commitment to promoting health and quality in food consumption.D√çAZ DE SANTOS:D√≠az de Santos is a prominent publishing company based in Spain, recognized for its extensive range of academic and professional publications. The company specializes in educational literature, with a particular focus on medical and nutritional topics. Among its notable works is the "Manual de Nutrici√≥n Cl√≠nica y Diet√©tica," which highlights its commitment to providing valuable resources in the field of nutrition. Overall, D√≠az de Santos is well-regarded for its contributions to both academic and professional literature, particularly in the areas of health and education.NUTRITION UNIT:The Nutrition Unit is a department within hospitals responsible for overseeing dietary plans and ensuring they meet patients' nutritional needs.ONCOLOGICAL TREATMENT:Oncological treatment refers to medical care provided to patients with cancer, which may require specific dietary considerations.DIABETIC DEBUT:Diabetic debut refers to the initial onset of diabetes in a patient, during which dietary education and management are crucial.FOOD ALLERGIES:Food allergies are adverse reactions to certain foods that must be documented and managed in a hospital setting to ensure patient safety.NUTRITIONAL SUPPLEMENTS:Nutritional supplements are additional nutrients provided to patients when their dietary intake is insufficient for their health needs. They are particularly recommended for patients post-bariatric surgery to ensure adequate protein and nutrient intake. These products are also intended to enhance the diet of individuals with specific health conditions, such as cancer and diabetes, to improve nutritional intake and support recovery. Nutritional supplements are commonly used in clinical settings for patients with specific nutritional needs, offering a means to provide essential vitamins, minerals, and trace elements that may be lacking in their regular diet. Overall, nutritional supplements play a crucial role in enhancing dietary intake and ensuring that individuals receive the necessary nutrients for optimal health.HYPERCHOLESTEROLEMIA:Hypercholesterolemia is a condition of elevated cholesterol levels in the blood, which may require dietary modifications.CARDIOVASCULAR ISSUES:Cardiovascular issues refer to health problems related to the heart and blood vessels, often requiring dietary adjustments.FOOD INTOLERANCES:Food intolerances refer to adverse reactions to specific foods that do not engage the immune system. These intolerances often require individuals to make dietary modifications to avoid discomfort and other related symptoms.CODES OF DIETS:Codes of diets are protocols established in hospitals that outline various dietary options available for patients.MANUAL DE NUTRICI√ìN Y METABOLISMO:Manual de Nutrici√≥n y Metabolismo is a publication that provides comprehensive information on nutrition and metabolism.MANUAL DE NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA:Manual de Nutrici√≥n Cl√≠nica y Diet√©tica is a publication that focuses on clinical nutrition and dietary practices.ALFONSO VIDAL CASARIEGO:Alfonso Vidal Casariego is a distinguished medical professional specializing in endocrinology and nutrition, with a significant focus on the treatment of obesity. He serves as a researcher in clinical nutrition at the Clinical Nutrition Unit of the University Hospital of Le√≥n. In addition to his clinical work, Alfonso Vidal Casariego is also an author in the field of clinical nutrition and dietetics, contributing valuable insights and knowledge to the discipline.ALICIA CALLEJA FERN√ÅNDEZ:Alicia Calleja Fern√°ndez is a medical professional specializing in endocrinology and nutrition, with a focus on the treatment of obesity. She is also a researcher in the field of clinical nutrition and dietetics, affiliated with the Clinical Nutrition Unit at the University Hospital of Le√≥n. Through her work, she contributes significantly to advancing knowledge and practices in nutrition and obesity management.GEORGIOS KYRIAKOS:Georgios Kyriakos is a medical professional specializing in endocrinology and nutrition, contributing to the treatment of obesity.TRATAMIENTO FARMACOL√ìGICO DE LA OBESIDAD:Tratamiento farmacol√≥gico de la obesidad refers to the pharmacological treatment of obesity, which is a topic covered in the document.TRATAMIENTO QUIR√öRGICO DE LA OBESIDAD:Tratamiento quir√∫rgico de la obesidad refers to surgical treatment 